---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Structuring Your Output - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell
    chapterId="competitive-research"
    subChapterId="structuring-your-output"
  >
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        You've learned how to set up research, write effective prompts, cite
        sources, and understand what AI can't do. Now it's time to package all
        of that knowledge into something reusable. Instead of remembering to
        include citation rules, verification steps, and limitation warnings
        every time you start a research task, you can give the AI a complete
        playbook and let it follow the process consistently.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two things turn research into a deliverable people actually use: the
        format, and making it repeatable. Start with the format.
      </p>

      <h3>Start With an HTML Artifact</h3>

      <p>
        Markdown is fine for notes, but it's a flat way to hand someone a
        report. An HTML file does more in a single document you open in a
        browser: a summary up top, comparison tables, sections you can collapse,
        a chart where it helps. In Claude these come back as artifacts, and
        you'll see them across the Claude app, Cowork, and Claude Code. For a
        competitive analysis, asking for an HTML report usually gets you
        something usable faster than fighting with markdown formatting. The{" "}
        <a href="/fundamentals/html">HTML chapter</a> goes into why output has
        moved this way. Claude handles PDFs well now too, which helps when the
        result needs to be printed or sent on.
      </p>

      <p>
        Get the artifact looking right first, then convert it. Once the shape is
        settled, you can ask for a Confluence page, a slide outline, a CSV, or
        an email version, and the AI adapts something that already exists more
        reliably than it invents every format at once. That conversion is worth
        making repeatable, which is what the rest of this page is about.
      </p>

      <p>
        The repeatable part is called a <strong>skill</strong>. Think of it as
        handing a specialist a detailed briefing document before they start
        work. The specialist already knows how to research, but the briefing
        tells them exactly how <em>you</em> want it done: what format to use,
        where to save things, how to cite sources, and what to watch out for.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="info" title="Good to know">
        <p>
          Skills follow the open <a href="https://agentskills.io" class="underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">agentskills.io</a> format, which means
          they work across tools that support the standard. For Claude-specific
          best practices, check the{" "}
          <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices" class="underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official skill best practices guide</a>.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>What is a Skill?</h3>

      <p>
        At its simplest, a skill is a folder containing a <code>SKILL.md</code>{" "}
        file. That file has two parts: YAML frontmatter (metadata about the
        skill) and a markdown body (the actual instructions). The AI reads the
        metadata to decide <em>when</em> to use the skill, and reads the body
        to know <em>how</em> to execute it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here's what a minimal skill folder looks like:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`.claude/skills/competitive-research/
├── SKILL.md
└── references/
    ├── report-template.md
    ├── source-quality-guide.md
    └── limitations-patterns.md`}</code></div>

      <p>
        The <code>SKILL.md</code> file is the main entry point. The{" "}
        <code>references/</code> folder holds supporting documents that the AI
        can pull in when needed. This is called <strong>progressive
        disclosure</strong>: the AI loads only the skill's core instructions
        at first, then loads reference files on demand. This keeps things fast
        and avoids filling up the context window with details that might not
        be relevant to every task.
      </p>

      <h3>Step-by-Step: Building a Competitive Research Skill</h3>

      <p>
        Let's build this skill piece by piece. Each step adds a new section
        to the <code>SKILL.md</code> file, and we'll explain the reasoning
        behind each decision.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 1: Define the Metadata</h3>

      <p>
        Every skill starts with YAML frontmatter. The two required fields are{" "}
        <code>name</code> and <code>description</code>. The name is
        human-readable, but the description does the heavy lifting: it's how
        the AI decides whether to activate this skill for a given task.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`---
name: Competitive Research
description: >-
  Use this skill whenever the user asks to research competitors,
  analyze market positioning, compare products or services,
  investigate industry trends, or evaluate competitive landscapes.
  Also use this when asked to create competitive analyses,
  market reports, or feature comparisons.
---`}</code></div>

      <p>
        Notice how the description is intentionally broad. It lists many
        possible trigger phrases rather than one narrow definition. This is
        the "pushy" approach to skill descriptions, and it's the right one:
        it's better for a skill to activate when it's <em>slightly</em>{" "}
        relevant than to miss the cases where it's needed.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
        <div class="border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">
            Good description
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-emerald-800 leading-relaxed">
            "Use this skill whenever the user asks to research competitors,
            analyze market positioning, compare products, investigate industry
            trends, or evaluate competitive landscapes."
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs text-emerald-600 mt-2">
            Broad, lists many trigger phrases, catches edge cases.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-red-200 bg-red-50 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-red-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">
            Bad description
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-red-800 leading-relaxed">
            "Competitive research skill."
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs text-red-600 mt-2">
            Too vague. The AI has no idea when to use it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Step 2: Write the Opening Context</h3>

      <p>
        Right after the frontmatter, write a paragraph that explains what the
        skill does and why it exists. This sets the AI's "mindset" for
        everything that follows. Think of it like the opening paragraph of a
        job posting: it tells the reader what they're about to do and why it
        matters.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`# Competitive Research

You are conducting competitive research that will inform real
business decisions. Accuracy and verifiability are more important
than comprehensiveness. It is better to report "I could not verify
this" than to include unverified claims. Every finding should be
traceable back to a specific source.

Your research will be read by product managers, designers, and
executives who will use it to guide strategy. Write for clarity
and actionability.`}</code></div>

      <p>
        A few things to notice here. First, we explain <em>why</em> accuracy
        matters ("will inform real business decisions") rather than just
        saying "be accurate." AI responds better to reasoning than to bare
        commands. Second, we explicitly set the priority: accuracy over
        comprehensiveness. Without this, the AI tends to fill reports with
        loosely verified claims to look thorough. Third, we tell it who the
        audience is so it can calibrate tone and detail level.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 3: Define the Workflow</h3>

      <p>
        This is the core of the skill. A clear, numbered workflow gives the AI
        a predictable process to follow. Without it, the AI will improvise a
        different approach every time, making results inconsistent.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`## Workflow

Follow these steps in order:

### 1. Clarify the Research Scope
Before starting any research, confirm with the user:
- [ ] Which competitors to focus on (or ask for guidance)
- [ ] What aspects to compare (features, pricing, positioning, etc.)
- [ ] What the research will be used for (presentation, strategy doc, etc.)
- [ ] Any specific questions they need answered

### 2. Create the Workspace
Set up the output folder structure (see Workspace Structure below).
This keeps research organized and makes it easy for others to find
specific pieces later.

### 3. Research Each Competitor
For each competitor:
- [ ] Gather information from official sources first (website, docs, blog)
- [ ] Cross-reference with third-party sources (reviews, news, analysis)
- [ ] Note the date of each source
- [ ] Flag any claims you cannot verify from primary sources
- [ ] Save raw notes to the competitor's file in the workspace

### 4. Analyze and Compare
- [ ] Identify patterns across competitors
- [ ] Build comparison matrices for key dimensions
- [ ] Note gaps in the market that no competitor addresses
- [ ] Highlight areas where our product is stronger or weaker

### 5. Deliver the Report
- [ ] Compile findings into the report template
- [ ] Include all source citations
- [ ] Add a limitations section noting what you couldn't verify
- [ ] Create an executive summary with key takeaways`}</code></div>

      <p>
        The checklist format (<code>- [ ]</code>) is intentional. It gives the
        AI a concrete set of items to work through and makes it easy for you
        to see if something was skipped. The workflow also front-loads
        clarification (Step 1) because research without clear scope tends to
        produce generic, unhelpful results.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
        <p>
          When writing workflows, explain the <em>why</em> alongside the{" "}
          <em>what</em>. "Create the workspace (this keeps research organized
          and makes it easy for others to find things)" is better than just
          "Create the workspace." The AI uses reasoning to make judgment calls,
          so giving it context for each step leads to better decisions in edge
          cases.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Step 4: Add the Workspace Structure</h3>

      <p>
        Defining where output goes might seem like a minor detail, but it
        makes a huge difference in practice. Without a defined structure, the
        AI will dump everything into a single file or scatter files
        unpredictably. A clear structure means anyone on the team can find
        what they need without asking where things ended up.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`## Workspace Structure

Create the following folder structure for each research project:

\`\`\`
competitive-research/
├── README.md              # Executive summary and key findings
├── report.md              # Full research report
├── competitors/
│   ├── competitor-a.md    # Raw notes for each competitor
│   ├── competitor-b.md
│   └── ...
├── comparisons/
│   ├── feature-matrix.md  # Side-by-side feature comparison
│   └── pricing-matrix.md  # Pricing comparison if applicable
└── sources.md             # Master list of all sources used
\`\`\`

Use kebab-case for all filenames. Replace "competitor-a" with the
actual company name (e.g., \`acme-corp.md\`).`}</code></div>

      <p>
        This gives the AI (and your teammates) a shared mental model for where
        things live. The <code>sources.md</code> file is especially important:
        it creates a single place where all citations are collected, making
        verification straightforward.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 5: Build in Quality Guardrails</h3>

      <p>
        This is arguably the most important section of the entire skill.
        Everything we covered in the{" "}
        <a href="/competitive-research/citing-sources">Citing Sources</a>{" "}
        and{" "}
        <a href="/competitive-research/limitations">Limitations</a>{" "}
        chapters gets encoded here as rules the AI must follow. Without these
        guardrails, the AI will produce confident-sounding reports with
        unchecked claims, exactly the kind of output that gets shared in a
        meeting and later turns out to be wrong.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`## Quality Standards

### Source Citation Rules
- Every factual claim must include a source URL or reference
- Prefer primary sources (company websites, official docs, SEC filings)
  over secondary sources (blog posts, news articles)
- Include the date you accessed each source
- If a claim comes from a single source, note that explicitly:
  "According to [Source], ..." rather than stating it as universal fact

### Verification Requirements
- Cross-reference key claims across at least two independent sources
- If you cannot verify a claim, mark it clearly:
  "[UNVERIFIED] Competitor X reportedly has 500 employees"
- Never present pricing as exact unless sourced from an official
  pricing page. Use "approximately" or "as of [date]" qualifiers.

### Limitations Disclosure
Every report must end with a "Limitations" section that includes:
- What you could NOT find information about
- Sources that were behind paywalls or login walls
- Information that may be outdated (note the most recent source date)
- Areas where your analysis is based on inference rather than direct
  evidence`}</code></div>

      <p>
        The <code>[UNVERIFIED]</code> tag pattern is particularly useful. It
        gives the AI explicit permission to include uncertain information (which
        might still be valuable) while making it impossible to accidentally
        treat it as confirmed. This is much better than the alternative, where
        the AI either leaves out uncertain-but-useful information entirely or
        presents it with false confidence.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="info" title="Skills tip">
        <p>
          Quality guardrails are the sections you should spend the most time
          on. The workflow tells the AI <em>what</em> to do, but guardrails
          tell it <em>how well</em> to do it. A skill with a great workflow
          but no guardrails will produce inconsistent quality. A skill with
          strong guardrails but a loose workflow will still produce reliable
          output.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Step 6: Add Reference Files for Progressive Disclosure</h3>

      <p>
        By now, the <code>SKILL.md</code> file is getting long. If we keep
        adding detail (report templates, source quality rubrics, limitation
        patterns), the file will become unwieldy and the AI will have to load
        all of it into context every time, even for simple tasks.
      </p>

      <p>
        The solution is <strong>reference files</strong>. These are separate
        markdown files in a <code>references/</code> folder that the AI loads
        only when it needs them. In the <code>SKILL.md</code>, you point to
        them with instructions like "see <code>references/report-template.md</code>{" "}
        for the full template."
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`.claude/skills/competitive-research/
├── SKILL.md                              # Core workflow and rules
└── references/
    ├── report-template.md                # Full report template with sections
    ├── source-quality-guide.md           # How to evaluate source reliability
    └── limitations-patterns.md           # Common limitation types to check for`}</code></div>

      <p>
        Then in your <code>SKILL.md</code>, you reference them like this:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`### Report Template
When compiling the final report, follow the structure in
\`references/report-template.md\`. Load that file before writing
the report.

### Source Evaluation
When assessing source quality, refer to the criteria in
\`references/source-quality-guide.md\`.

### Limitations Checklist
Before finalizing any report, review the common limitation
patterns in \`references/limitations-patterns.md\` to make sure
you haven't missed anything.`}</code></div>

      <p>
        This approach keeps your <code>SKILL.md</code> focused on the
        workflow and guardrails while putting detailed reference material
        where it can be loaded on demand. The AI only pulls in the report
        template when it's actually writing the report, not when it's still
        in the research phase.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="info" title="Good to know">
        <p>
          A good rule of thumb: if your <code>SKILL.md</code> is over 500 lines,
          you should probably move some content into reference files. The core
          skill file should be scannable in a few minutes. Details, templates,
          and extended examples belong in references.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>The Complete Skill</h3>

      <p>
        Here's a summary of the final skill structure and what each file
        handles:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`.claude/skills/competitive-research/
│
├── SKILL.md
│   ├── Frontmatter: name, description (broad trigger phrases)
│   ├── Opening context: purpose, audience, accuracy priority
│   ├── Workflow: 5-step process with checklists
│   ├── Workspace structure: folder layout for output
│   ├── Quality standards: citation rules, verification, limitations
│   └── Reference pointers: links to detailed reference files
│
└── references/
    ├── report-template.md      # Sections, headings, and placeholder
    │                             content for the final deliverable
    ├── source-quality-guide.md  # Rubric for evaluating source
    │                             reliability (primary vs secondary,
    │                             recency, authority)
    └── limitations-patterns.md  # Checklist of common things AI
                                  # research misses (paywalls,
                                  # real-time data, regional info)`}</code></div>

      <p>
        Rather than reproducing the entire skill here (it would be quite
        long), the key sections are the ones we walked through above. You
        can find a complete working version of this skill in the project's{" "}
        <code>.claude/skills/competitive-research/</code> folder to use
        as a starting point for your own skills.
      </p>

      <h3>Real-World Example: The Skill in Action</h3>

      <p>
        To test this skill, we ran it against a real research question:
        feature flagging tools for small and medium-sized Canadian banks.
        The results show both how the skill structures output and how
        prompt quality affects what you get back.
      </p>

      <h4 class="text-base font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">The Prompt Matters</h4>

      <p>
        Here's what someone might naturally type into an AI chat:
      </p>

      <Callout variant="warning" title="Original prompt">
        <p class="italic">
          "We need a competitive analysis of what small &amp; medium sized banks
          in Canada are using for feature flagging as well as who the market
          leaders are for feature flagging tools"
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        This prompt has several problems based on what we covered in{" "}
        <a href="/competitive-research/setting-up-your-research">Setting Up Your Research</a>:
        no specific strategic questions, no company context, no dimensions
        to compare, no audience specified, and it doesn't ask the AI to
        search the web. Even with a good skill, a vague prompt produces
        vague results.
      </p>

      <p>Here's the revised version that follows the rules from earlier chapters:</p>

      <Callout variant="success" title="Improved prompt">
          <p class="italic">
            "We're evaluating feature flagging tools for a product that serves
            small and medium-sized banks in Canada. We need to understand the
            competitive landscape to inform our build-vs-buy decision and
            potentially position our own feature flagging capability.
          </p>
          <p class="italic mt-2">
            Please search the web for current information on each of these
            questions:
          </p>
          <ol class="italic mt-2 ml-4 space-y-1 list-decimal">
            <li>What feature flagging tools are the market leaders right now?</li>
            <li>Which of these tools are actually being used by Canadian banks
              or financial institutions? Look for case studies, compliance
              certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001, Canadian data residency)</li>
            <li>What are the key differentiators? (pricing model, self-hosted
              vs cloud, SDK support, audit logging, compliance features)</li>
            <li>Are there any Canada-specific or banking-specific solutions?</li>
            <li>What are the typical pain points banks face with feature
              flagging adoption?</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="italic mt-2">
            The audience is our product and engineering leadership team.
            Deliverable should be a comparison matrix + executive summary."
          </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        The revised prompt gives the AI a decision context (build-vs-buy),
        five specific questions, dimensions to compare (pricing, compliance,
        deployment model), an audience (leadership), and a deliverable
        format. It also explicitly asks the AI to search the web.
      </p>

      <h4 class="text-base font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">What the Skill Produced</h4>

      <p>
        Running the improved prompt through the competitive research skill
        produced a complete research workspace with 7 competitors analyzed,
        each with individual overview, features, pricing, and source files.
        Here's the folder structure it created:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto my-6 whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>{`competitive-research-feature-flags/
├── README.md
├── competitors/
│   ├── launchdarkly/
│   │   ├── overview.md, features.md, pricing.md, sources.md
│   ├── split-io-harness/
│   ├── flagsmith/
│   ├── unleash/
│   ├── cloudbees/
│   ├── statsig/
│   └── devcycle/
├── analysis/
│   ├── comparison-matrix.md
│   └── key-findings.md
└── deliverables/
    ├── executive-summary.md
    └── comparison-matrix.csv`}</code></div>

      <p>
        Some highlights from the output that show the skill's guardrails
        working:
      </p>

      <ul class="space-y-3 my-4">
        <li>
          <strong>Source discipline held up.</strong> Every competitor file
          includes inline citations with access dates, and each has a
          dedicated <code>sources.md</code> with tiered source classification.
          Claims that couldn't be web-verified are tagged{" "}
          <code>[BASED ON TRAINING DATA - VERIFY]</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Limitations are honest.</strong> The executive summary
          explicitly notes that enterprise pricing required sales contact,
          no hands-on product testing was done, and no specific Canadian bank
          customer references were found for any vendor.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The analysis is actionable.</strong> Rather than just listing
          features, the comparison matrix includes a "Canadian Banking
          Suitability Score" that weighs the dimensions that actually matter
          for the decision (data residency, compliance, self-hosted options).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The recommendation is clear.</strong> The executive summary
          leads with a bottom line ("self-hosted open-source tools are the
          strongest buy options") and gives five specific next steps.
        </li>
      </ul>

