      <h3>It Still Needs a Human</h3>

      <p>
        Even with a well-structured skill and a strong prompt, the output is a{" "}
        <em>draft</em>, not a finished product. The skill gets you 80% of the
        way there fast, but the last 20% requires someone who actually knows
        the domain. Here are a couple of things from the executive summary
        above that a knowledgeable human would catch and improve:
      </p>

      <ul class="space-y-3 my-4">
        <li>
          <strong>The "do not build from scratch" recommendation assumes too much.</strong>{" "}
          The AI treats this as a clean greenfield decision, but someone on
          the team might know there's already an internal system that handles
          configuration toggles, or a platform component that could be
          extended to support feature flags without starting from zero. We
          also might have missed a constraint in the prompt: maybe the
          company has a policy against open-source dependencies in production,
          or there's a procurement freeze that makes "buy" impractical right
          now. A human reviewer would reframe the recommendation around what
          actually exists internally and what's realistically on the table.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The data residency constraint might not be a dealbreaker.</strong>{" "}
          The report treats Canadian data residency as a hard requirement
          that eliminates most cloud vendors. But with the help of legal
          counsel, there may be a path forward through careful data modeling:
          if the feature flag payloads contain no personally identifiable
          information or regulated data, the residency requirement might not
          apply to this specific system. A human who understands the
          regulatory landscape (or who can loop in someone who does) could
          turn this from "eliminates all cloud options" into "cloud options
          are viable if we structure the data correctly."
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Missing competitors the AI wouldn't know about.</strong>{" "}
          Someone working in Canadian banking might know about niche or
          regional tools that don't rank well in web searches. The AI can
          only find what's indexed and visible. A human reviewer familiar
          with the industry would add any missing players and flag whether
          the comparison matrix is actually complete.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The pattern here is simple: use the skill to handle the repetitive,
        structured parts (gathering, organizing, formatting, citing), then
        have a human apply domain knowledge, verify key claims, and add
        context the AI can't access. The skill doesn't replace expertise. It
        frees up your experts to focus on the parts that actually require
        judgment.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
        <p>
          You can absolutely ask the AI to iterate on its output: "make the
          recommendations less absolute," "add a caveat about internal
          tooling," "rewrite the executive summary for a less technical
          audience." Use the AI for the back-and-forth refinement, but do
          the finishing touches yourself. Make sure the tone matches how you
          actually communicate, adjust any conclusions that don't reflect
          your understanding of the situation, and add the context only you
          have. The AI gets you to a strong draft fast. The final version
          should sound like it came from you.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Tips for Writing Your Own Skills</h3>

      <p>
        The competitive research skill is just one example. The same structure
        works for anything you do repeatedly: writing requirements documents,
        creating test plans, conducting code reviews, drafting presentations.
        Here are some guidelines for building your own.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Start with a workflow you repeat often.</strong> If you find
        yourself giving the AI the same instructions across multiple
        conversations, that's a skill waiting to be written. Common candidates
        include: writing Jira tickets, creating Confluence pages, building
        slide outlines, drafting emails to stakeholders, and reviewing pull
        requests.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Explain why, not just what.</strong> "Cross-reference claims
        across two sources" is a fine instruction, but "Cross-reference claims
        across two sources because single-source claims are more likely to be
        inaccurate or biased" is better. The AI uses reasoning to handle
        ambiguous situations, so giving it the rationale behind your rules
        helps it make good judgment calls in cases your instructions don't
        explicitly cover.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Test and iterate.</strong> Your first version won't be
        perfect. Run a few real tasks through the skill, look at what it gets
        wrong, and update the instructions. Pay special attention to the
        places where the AI makes assumptions you didn't expect. Those are
        the spots where you need clearer guidance.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Keep SKILL.md under 500 lines.</strong> If it's longer,
        break content into reference files. The core file should be a clear,
        scannable overview of the workflow and quality standards. Detailed
        templates, extended examples, and reference material belong in
        separate files.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Use templates for output format consistency.</strong> If your
        skill produces a specific deliverable (a report, a ticket, a
        document), include a template in the references folder. Templates
        prevent the AI from inventing a new format every time and make it
        easy for your team to know what to expect.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
        <p>
          If you use Claude Code, check out the built-in skill creator
          workflow. You can describe what you want the skill to do, run
          test prompts against it, and iterate on the instructions until the
          output matches your expectations. It's the fastest way to go from
          "I want a skill that does X" to a working version.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        Skills are one of the highest-leverage things you can build with AI
        tools. A good skill turns hours of repeated explanation into a single
        command. And because they're just markdown files in a folder, they're
        easy to share with your team, version control alongside your code,
        and improve over time.
      </p>
